      },
      "worked": "The maximum selects the larger of $x$ and $x^2$. Both expressions are defined for every real $x$, so $D=(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2-x&=x(x-1)\\\\x^2-x&\\ge0\\quad\\text{for }x\\le0\\text{ or }x\\ge1.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThus\n$$f(x)=\\begin{cases}x^2,&x\\le0\\text{ or }x\\ge1,\\\\x,&0<x<1.\\end{cases}$$\n\nThe maximum is always non-negative because $x^2\\ge0$, and $f(0)=0$. On $x\\le0$, the branch $f(x)=x^2$ takes every value in $[0,\\infty)$. Therefore,\n$$R=[0,\\infty).$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
